## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about multi-key sorting, stability, and practical trade-offs in beginner-friendly tasks. It also aligns with real-world data processing where groupings matter.

## Key Concepts

- Sorting stability and its impact on multi-key ordering
- Two-pass stable sorting technique
- Time complexity: O(n log n) for mergesort
- Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary memory

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableMergeSortBy(arr, key) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2)
  const left = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(0, mid), key)
  const right = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(mid), key)
  const out = []
  let i = 0, j = 0
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i][key] <= right[j][key]) out.push(left[i++])
    else out.push(right[j++])
  }
  return out.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j))
}

function sortRecords(arr) {
  const byTime = stableMergeSortBy(arr, 'time')
  return stableMergeSortBy(byTime, 'priority')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify if you must sort in place and the library sort is not stable?
